      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e but I believe that the fundamental issue with `check_edge_against_rect` would also present itself for quadratic curves\n\nSince the first direction of the quadratic curve with the same starting point and end point is `kUnknow`, it will not be affected by `check_edge_against_rect` because the method `SkPath::conservativelyContainsRect` will return early\n```\n    SkPathFirstDirection direction \u003d SkPathPriv::ComputeFirstDirection(*this);\n    if (direction \u003d\u003d SkPathFirstDirection::kUnknown) {\n        return false;\n    }\n```\n\n------------------------------------------------------\n\n\u003e Should we always chop the cubic at 1/3 and 2/3 to avoid this issue and benefit from more accurate contains checks?\n\nSince we don\u0027t know how many cubic segments there are in this path. For those convex paths composed of very many cubic segments, chop cubic will bring extra overhead, but the benefits are minimal.\n\n------------------------------------------------------\n\n\u003e OR since this is a conservative contains check, is this extra overhead worthwhile? Instead of checking !AllPointsEq should we check if prevPt !\u003d nextPt and remember that if we never encountered a non-zero edge then the contains check should be false?\n\nI believe that only the case in the issue here will satisfy \"we never encountered a non-zero edge\", because Line, Quad and Conic with the same starting point and end point will return early because the first direction is `kUnkown`.\n\nMaybe we just need to put a simple patch and return `false` when `SkPathVerb::kCubic \u003d\u003d verb \u0026\u0026 prevPt \u003d\u003d pts[nextPt]`",
      "parentUuid": "c7a50f29_eb030514",
      "revId": "6aee59c5d4944ae0ae507aabec449e656914176c",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
